<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Two keys, and this file exists only to carry them. Both are keys Xcode
         does NOT synthesize, which is the rule this file is built on.

         GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE is YES for this target, exactly as it is for
         FarCoolerWatch: every other key in the built bundle —
         CFBundleIdentifier, CFBundleDisplayName, the version pair,
         UIDeviceFamily — is synthesized from a build setting, and Xcode MERGES
         those into this file rather than replacing it. A file that defined a
         key Xcode also synthesizes would be a second answer to one question; a
         file that defines only keys Xcode knows nothing about cannot collide
         with the generated set at all. See FarCoolerWatch/Info.plist, where the
         same shape was measured on a built bundle.

         NSExtension is what makes this a WidgetKit extension rather than any
         other kind, and there is no INFOPLIST_KEY_ for it — it is a dictionary,
         and INFOPLIST_KEY_<key> round-trips one scalar string per key. There is
         deliberately no NSExtensionPrincipalClass and there must not be:
         WidgetKit finds the @main WidgetBundle itself, and naming a class here
         makes the extension fail to load with no symptom other than a
         complication that is missing from the face gallery.

         FarCoolerAppGroup is here rather than as
         INFOPLIST_KEY_FarCoolerAppGroup because that setting DOES NOT WORK, and
         does not work quietly: Xcode honors INFOPLIST_KEY_<key> only for keys
         it already knows, and drops a custom one with no warning and no error.
         Measured twice on this project — Task 2 saw the synthesized plist come
         out as literally `{}`.

         It is worse here than it was there. `Bundle.main` inside an .appex is
         the EXTENSION's own bundle, so this process cannot read the watch app's
         merged plist even in principle, however well that plist is filled in.
         What it costs if this is ever "simplified" back to a build setting:
         `SnapshotStore.groupIdentifier` reads this key by name and returns nil
         when it is missing, so `SnapshotStore.read()` answers "nothing known" —
         the identical answer it gives when no snapshot has ever arrived. The
         complication draws "Open …" forever against a watch whose app is
         receiving the fleet perfectly well, and nothing in any log tells the
         two apart.

         `$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)` and not a literal: four channels, four groups,
         and this is the same expression the entitlement beside it expands, so
         the container the code asks for and the container the signature grants
         cannot come to differ. -->
    <key>NSExtension</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
        <string>com.apple.widgetkit-extension</string>
    </dict>
    <key>FarCoolerAppGroup</key>
    <string>$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)</string>
</dict>
</plist>
